.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1224 PM PDT Thu Oct 1 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

Ridging, ridging, ridging... a basic summary of the weather pattern
across the western United States in the short and medium terms. We
are in the middle of an extended period of temperatures warmer than
seasonal averages and rather compressed marine layers, meaning that
the stratus deck will remain confined to the immediate coast during
the daytime and expand into the North Bay Valleys, the SF and
Monterey Bay regions, and the northern Salinas Valley overnight, but
keep the interior valleys generally clear. High temperatures today
and Friday range from the middle 80s to middle 90s inland, the upper
90s across the warmest spots of the interior Central Coast, the
middle 70s to middle 80s near the Bays, and the middle 60s to lower
70s at the Pacific coast. The onshore breeze will be rather light
with gusts of 20 to 25 mph possible through favored gaps and passes
and the northern Salinas Valley in the afternoon and evening hours.

Regions of Moderate HeatRisk are expanding across the inland regions
today and Friday. These correspond to a moderate risk of heat-
related illnesses for sensitive populations, including children, the
elderly, pregnant women, people with chronic illnesses, and people
who work or live outdoors without access to adequate cooling or
hydration. Follow these tips to avoid heat exhaustion and heatstroke:

* Stay hydrated and drink plenty of fluids.
* Wear lightweight, light-colored clothing.
* Reduce time spent outdoors or stay in the shade.
* Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles.
* Use sunscreen if going to the coast or the pool.

Minor coastal flooding is possible across the bayshore locations of
San Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay around high tide.
Up to 1.1 feet of inundation above normally dry land is expected,
including up to 0.7 ft of surge caused by thermal expansion and sea
level rise since the sea level datum (basically the point where we
define as "sea level") was set in the 1983-2001 period. At the San
Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.1 ft above
normal (6.92 ft MLLW) at 2:58 PM on Thursday 10/1 and 0.9 ft above
normal (6.71 ft MLLW) at 3:58 PM on Friday 10/2.

