.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1224 PM PDT Thu Oct 1 2026
(Saturday through next Wednesday)

As the upper level ridge starts to amplify over the western United
States, a noticeable warmup this weekend will cause inland highs to
rise into the middle 90s to near 103, while the Bays see highs
across the 80s to the lower 90s, creating hazardous heat conditions
for anyone without adequate cooling or hydration. Mild overnight
lows in the upper 60s to middle 70s across the higher elevations and
the middle to upper 60s in the interior East and South Bays will
limit the opportunity for the body to recover overnight. As a
result, a Heat Advisory has been issued from 11 AM Saturday to 10 PM
Sunday across the Sonoma Coastal Range, the North Bay Interior
Mountains, the San Francisco Bay Shoreline, the East Bay and South
Bay, the Santa Cruz Mountains, the Santa Lucia Range, the interior
mountains of Monterey and San Benito Counties, and the southern
Salinas Valley. Meanwhile, locations along the Pacific Coast will
feel the influence of a highly compressed marine layer, with highs
in the upper 60s to middle 70s and lows in the upper 50s to lower
60s.

Towards the beginning of the next work week, the ridge begins to
flatten and temperatures may cool by a couple of degrees, but
chances for hazardous heat remain high and the Heat Advisory may A
slight cooling of a couple of degrees at most is possible towards
the next work week as the ridge begins to flatten, but need to be
extended as the forecast comes into better view. Ensemble model
cluster analysis converges on another amplification of the ridge
towards the later part of the next work week, and things get
interesting again for the second weekend of October (the 10th and
11th). The clusters begin to split into scenarios that keep the
ridging in place over the western United States and those that show
more zonal flow or even a trough coming into the West Coast. This
split between the cluster scenarios will be interesting to look at
through the week, and is part of the reason why forecasts beyond the
next seven days are more often discussed in terms of probabilities
than deterministic language.

